   "subject": "What do you do if you can't identify to species level?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Good morning, <div><br></div><div>   I&#39;m learning how to use CamtrapR and am still a novice. One subject I&#39;ve yet to see addressed here is what, exactly, do you do if you can&#39;t identify to species level? Can you still use this program? Can you add this to your hierarchical tree? How does this change the dynamics/scripts?</div><div><br></div><div>Best, </div><div>MB</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "7Hf5X1-JCAAJ",
   "from": "Hugo C. M. Costa",
   "author": "283c96b6b420b097",
   "date": "2019-05-29T21:14:12Z",
   "subject": "Re: What do you do if you can't identify to species level?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\">Hello Mabel, </div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-family:tahoma,sans-serif;font-size:small\">Yes, You can use camtrapR without identifying to species level, you can name your folder or tag your photo whatever you want (e.g Unidentified species, Genus spp, Species sp) and run the code normally. </div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
